  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: c18dce35c4f218942974316577a84ef5, type: 3}
  m_Name: Scene05_Context
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::OhmsLaw.SceneAgentContext
  sceneId: Scene05
  sceneTitle: Journey Inside the Wire
  askWattsonEnabled: 1
  covered:
  - 'Everything from Scene 4: the Christmas-lights mystery, Georg Ohm, the words
    voltage and current, the Ohm-niscope.'
  - Inside a metal the charge carriers are free electrons.
  - Before a battery is connected the electrons move randomly in every direction
    with no net drift.
  - A battery creates a potential difference (voltage) between two points; voltage
    is the electrical push. Symbol V, unit volt (V).
  - "When the battery is connected the electric field is set up along the whole circuit
    almost at once and all the electrons drift together, from the \u2212 terminal
    end toward the + terminal end."
  - Charge crossing a section of the wire each second is electric current. Symbol
    I, unit ampere (A).
  - "Conventional current is taken from + to \u2212; electrons drift from \u2212
    to +."
  - 'The scene shows only a small, evenly spread sample of the free electrons so
    one can be followed; a real wire has billions of billions. The drift shown is
    a picture: real drift is very slow (fractions of a millimetre per second) and
    much slower than the random motion, while the field is set up along the wire
    almost at once.'
  doNotSpoil:
  - How V and I are related
  - V is proportional to I
  - V = IR
  - R = V / I
  - Resistance
  - Ohms
  - The name 'Ohm's law'
  - The answer to the Christmas-lights mystery
  faq: {fileID: 11400000, guid: e545d3c62bba2464d923592aa07c0cce, type: 2}
